# Build context trim for `docker build -f docker/Dockerfile .` (root context, whole reactor).
#
# docker/Dockerfile deliberately does `COPY . .` rather than listing modules: the reactor builds
# `service`'s dependency on `qits-ci-domain` from source (it is never published), and a module list
# would have to be edited every time a module is added. This file is what makes that cheap — it
# drops everything the build does not need, so adding a module stays free and no stale output leaks
# in.

.git
.gitignore

# Maven / Quarkus build output — regenerated inside the build stage, and it is the bulk of a working
# tree here (two modules' target/, one of them holding a native binary and its build artifacts).
# Copying it in would also risk a stale target/ shadowing what the stage compiles.
**/target
**/target-ide

# The Angular client's install output: the bulk of a working tree once the submodule is initialised,
# full of binaries compiled for the HOST rather than for the builder image, and nothing in the build
# stage reads it — that stage runs no install and no client build (see docker/Dockerfile). `.angular`
# is the CLI's own cache, the same shape of host-local junk.
#
# NOTE THE ASYMMETRY with what this file used to say, and with the platform's Quinoa recipe
# (docs/project-setup-quinoa-angular.md), which excludes the client's dist/ as well: here dist/ is
# the PAYLOAD. Since the webui took a dependency on @qits/ui-components — which exists only on the
# platform's own npm registry, reachable from no address a docker build has — the bundle is built
# BEFORE this image by .config/qits/ci-post-receive.yml, on qits-net where that registry answers.
# Excluding dist/ would fail the build at the `test -f` guard on the mvnw line.
**/node_modules
service/src/main/webui/.angular

# Quinoa's own toolchain install directory. The build stage creates its own (service/.quinoa); a
# host-installed one would be a Linux/glibc question we do not want to import from whatever built the
# context.
**/.quinoa

# Local env / IDE cruft
.env
.idea
.vscode
.sdkmanrc

# Docs and the docker/ dir itself: nothing in the build reads them, and keeping them out means
# editing this Dockerfile or a README does not bust the `COPY . .` layer.
*.md
docs
docker
